    "resolved_question_text": "The following figure is a micrograph taken by a student while observing mitosis in onion root tip cells. Question: How much is the number of chromosomes in Jia compared with that in Yi?",
    "resolved_answer": "1/2",
    "resolved_from_fallback": true,
    "final_cot": "Image description: In the micrograph, onion root tip cells can be seen, and on the right there are two arrows labeled \"Jia\" and \"Yi\". In the cell indicated by Jia, darkly stained chromosomes can be seen concentrated or arranged, but they have not clearly separated into two groups moving toward the two poles; the darkly stained chromosomes in the cell indicated by Yi appear to have already separated into two groups, consistent with the characteristics of anaphase of mitosis. Reasoning: In prophase or metaphase of mitosis, the chromosome number in the cell is still 2n; by anaphase, because the centromeres split, sister chromatids separate and become independent chromosomes, and the chromosome number in the cell temporarily doubles to 4n. Therefore, the ratio of the chromosome numbers in Jia and Yi is 2n:4n=1:2, so the chromosome number in Jia is 1/2 of that in Yi.",
    "claims": [],
